# Who may publish what, Project 18.
#
# The username here is the certificate's common name, because
# use_identity_as_username is on. So every line below is enforced against
# something the private CA vouched for, rather than against a string the
# client chose.
#
# THE RULE IS MECHANICAL: A DEVICE WRITES UNDER ITS OWN NAME.
#
#   bench/<common name>/...
#
# That is a rule rather than a list, and the difference matters. A list of
# hand-assigned prefixes drifts: somebody adds a device, picks a prefix
# that looks free, and the ACL no longer says anything a reader can check.
# With the rule, the prefix is derived from the identity the certificate
# already carries, and tests/broker-config-test.sh asserts it mechanically
# for every user rather than one rule at a time.
#
# It also removes a whole class of mistake. Two devices cannot be given
# overlapping subtrees by accident, because their names are already
# distinct: the certificate authority refuses to issue two certificates
# with the same common name into the same ACL slot, and if it did the
# second device would simply be the first one.
#
# READ THE DIRECTION OF THESE RULES.
#
# Publishers write and do not read. That is the whole practical benefit of
# giving each device its own certificate: a sensor that is compromised can
# lie about itself and learn nothing about anything else. Written the other
# way round, with readwrite everywhere, the PKI would still authenticate
# everyone correctly and one broken device would see the entire bench.
# Decision 112.
#
# There is no topic line outside a user block, so there is no default
# permission: an identity not named here connects and can do nothing.

# Project 17's BLE gateway, which today publishes to a localhost broker
# that its own README calls a test fixture.
#
# ITS TOPIC HAS TO BE SET WHEN IT IS POINTED AT THIS BROKER. That project
# publishes to "bench/stwin" by default, which is not under its own name
# and would be refused here. The knob already exists and is one line:
# STWIN_TOPIC in meta-bench/recipes-bench/bench-stwin/files/stwin.conf,
# which becomes bench/ble-gateway/stwin. Nothing in that project is
# changed from here; the requirement is recorded in docs/PROTOCOL.md and
# the change belongs to whoever connects the two.
user ble-gateway
topic write bench/ble-gateway/#

# Project 20's keystore verifier publishes a value and subscribes for the
# answer, so this one genuinely needs both directions. It is the exception,
# and naming it as one is the point: an exception in a file is visible,
# an exception implied by a default is not.
user keystore-verify
topic readwrite bench/keystore-verify/#

# The ESP32 station. Confined to its own name, so a second station cannot
# overwrite the first one's readings.
user esp32-01
topic write bench/esp32-01/#

# The laptop, for looking. Read only: a subscriber that can also publish is
# a debugging tool that can inject a reading into somebody's results.
#
# The only identity whose prefix is not its own name, because its job is to
# see everything. It is also the only one that cannot write.
user bench-laptop
topic read bench/#
